        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe spec is implemented coherently (Soundex is a clean, well-placed `util` class used by both the model and service; the service filters read naturally; the javadoc/openapi updates track the behaviour change). But the change introduces avoidable duplication:\n\n**SHA-256-hex boilerplate duplicated within `Owner.java`.** Before this change `getIdentityKey()` was pure string concatenation. It now hashes, and in doing so it copies, almost verbatim, the try/catch + `MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\")` + `String.format(\"%02x\")` loop + `IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 not available\")` block that already sits immediately below it in `getHouseholdId()` (`Owner.java:270-285` vs `Owner.java:296-311`). The two methods now differ only in output length (full 64-char digest vs. first 6 bytes). This is a textbook extract-method: a single private helper, e.g.\n\n```java\nprivate static String sha256Hex(String value, int bytes) { ... }\n```\n\nwith `getIdentityKey()` calling `sha256Hex(key, 32)` and `getHouseholdId()` calling `sha256Hex(key, 6)`, would remove the copy and keep the two derived keys visibly consistent. As it stands, the same hashing bug would have to be fixed in two adjacent places.\n\n(Secondary, worth noting but not blocking: `ClinicServiceImpl.hash8()` at line 296 is a third copy of the same idiom. Not introduced by this diff, but if a shared hex-digest helper is extracted it's the natural third caller. Leaving it is defensible since it lives in a different class with a different casing/width.)\n\nEverything else \u2014 the removal of the household-duplicate 409 path, reusing `getIdentityKey()` for the soft-match \"different key\" check, and the narrowed `normalizeForComparison` doc comment \u2014 lands in the right units and reads idiomatically."
